\begin_layout Title
Runge-Kutta 12(10) Description
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The RK12(10) builds upon previous Runge-Kutta methodologies to estimate
 the local truncation error, and correspondingly changes the step size to
 cause the truncation errors to be more uniform.
 By using an embedded 10th-order method to compare results with the 12th-order
 method, the local truncation error may be estimated at the end of each
 step.
 This method's ability to control the step size allows for applications
 requiring a high accuracy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
C++ Code Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the rk1210() function is called, it performs an integration of the
 differential equations given in the procedure "deriv" (currently applied
 to the 2-D, two-body problem).
 The function allows the user to specify the initial time, final time, initial
 state values, initial step size, max step size, tolerance of local truncation
 error, printout options (print values at each step or interpolated values
 for t in the last step), and an option to have the step size be fixed.
 The tolerance is set to the input tolerance divided by 10.
 The variables istep and rstep count the number of The values of x are determine
d using the equations of condition and corresponding coefficients determiend
 by Fegain.
 Next, the step size is adjusted based on the error estimate and results
 are printed out to the user.
 Iterpolated values of 7th order may be calculated and printed for values
 of t within the last step using the interpolate() frunction; these interpolate
 values are calculated for the specified mystep (currently set to 0.1 in
 terms of t).
